Subject: [#94-1-430] Self Awareness is Bunk

John K Clark responds on self-awareness:

>>By this logic a Jupiter-sized brain will naturally be
>>vastly more  self-aware than we are.
>    
> I don't agree. The internal representation may be vastly more
> complex than our own but what it is trying to model (itself) is
> also vastly more complex. I think self-awareness is a function
> of the complexity of the internal representation of a mind
> DIVIDED by the complexity of the mind.

That's an interesting point.  Perhaps the concept of self-awareness (SA)
will need diversifing into two subconcepts:
  SA1 = amount of self-comprehension (My definition)
  SA2 = SA1/mental complexity (John's definition).
For they both seem perfectly valid and useful measures of SA.  There
would be limit to the level of SA2 any organism could attain, but no
limit to SA1 given arbitrary mental complexity.

> I agree. A Jupiter brain would be far more advanced than we are,
> far more aware than we are ,but it wouldn't be far more self
> aware [SA2] than we are.

I agree.

