From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Date: Sun, 23 Jan 94 14:58:34 GMT
Subject: [#94-1-431] Extropianism is *not* a religion.

Max More says:

> I will always welcome *reasonable* and contentful feedback on 
> the Extropian Principles and on the question of how to avoid 
> creeping dogma in our movement.

Here's some specific feedback.  (I posted a similar note to the list 
on faith a while back but received no feedback from Max.  Here goes 
again, in case Max missed it.)  All the quotes below are from the 
Principles 2.5 (July 1993) from our current welcome message.

> Faith in a better future is confidence that an external force, 
> whether God, State, or extraterrestrials, will solve our problems.

This notion that faith is somehow irrational is discussed (and trounced)
in _Atheism: the case against God_ by George(?) Smith.  [I may have the
title and author slightly wrong, but Max should remember it - he lent
it to me :-) ]  In it faith was discussed and shown to be like the web
of trust envisaged in crypto-key rings for the distribution of public
keys.  I have faith that the sun will rise tomorrow not because I have
personally made observations and calculated its path (relative to the
Earth) but because I am confident that lots of people more competent
than me, with more time to spare, have done the task for me.  We all
form judgements about the reliability of people in some areas (based on
our own knowledge) and are prepared to accept that they probably know
what they're talking about in other areas of which we know less.

The entire modern scientific process is based on faith.  I have
_critical_ faith in stuff I read in refereed journals.  A "good" journal
establishes a reputation in the scientific market-place by carefully
validating articles with reputable reviewers and attracting good quality
work.  Scientists build up their reputations by publishing in respected
journals.  The loop is closed.  The whole process relies on the rational
use of faith.

Doctrinal religions twist and pervert the use of _uncritical_ faith for
their own purposes.  Let's not be fooled by them.  Faith is an important
component in the reality forming process - we shouldn't eschew it just
because of the way religions behave.  Setting up faith as some bogeyman
looks very unconvincing.  Nothing turns me off a creed or lowers the
reputation of a speaker more than when it or they redefine words in a
narrow prejudicial way to make some rhetorical point.  (This a major
reason why I'm uncomfortable with Objectivism and the like, but that's
another story...)

>  Faith, or the Pollyanna/Dr. Pangloss variety of optimism, breeds
> passivity by promising progress as a gift bestowed on us by superior
> forces.  But, in return for the gift, faith requires a fixed belief
> in and supplication to external forces, thereby creating dogmatic
> beliefs and irrationally rigid behavior.

Again, that's not faith you're talking about, but _uncritical_ faith. 
I have faith in lots of things.  So do you.

> Extropians recognize the unique place of our species, and our
> opportunity to advance nature's evolution to new peaks. Beginning as
> mindless matter, parts of nature developed in a slow evolutionary
> ascendence, leading to progressively more powerful brains.  

This view of evolution that says we are the result of a slow but 
certain ascendance is questionable.  Other interpretations say that we
(intelligent, technological life) are a freak accident.  Consider:  
For most of evolutional history life was a composed of prokaryote 
sub-cellular components.  For all we know the transition to cellular
eukaryote life (where cells contain specialised components like
mitochondria or chloroplasts) formed by active symbiosis was a 
1-in-a-billion fluke.  Same with the development of primitive
nervous systems in animals.  Within the plant and other kingdoms no such
development took place, despite the much greater biomass-time of plants.
If a slow development of greater intelligence in plants was present then
I would accept the inevitability of intelligences as the end product of
evolution.  But we don't see this.  The higher plants have remained 
steadfastly stupid, sessile and very successful.

Any developments which took place _only_ in our ancestral forms must be
viewed as potentially very unlikely.  To look back over our evolutionary
history and make inferences about evolutionary progression from key
events in our ancestors is dangerous and logically false.  For example,
the certainty of the evolution of primate-level intelligence from the
average mammalian brain is questionable.  Dinosaurs and other giant
reptiles (which were probably warm-blooded and just active as mammals)
were dominant for far longer than mammals have been.  They reached a
similar level of encephalisation as non-primate mammals, yet they never
progressed any higher.

The abilities of chimps and other apes to pick up a residual sign
language suggests that our common simian ancestor had some linguistic
ability, but that this talent has since atrophied in the other ape
species.  Neanderthals had a _larger_ brain than modern humans and are
believed to have been an adaption to the ice age (much as the mammoth
was the elephants answer to the cold).  Perhaps our cranial capacity is
due to our neanderthal genes from the ice-age.  Glaciation is believed
to be an infrequent occurrence - is it a co-incidence that sapience
choose one of the brief and geologically short periods of glaciation to
emerge?

All this is not overwhelmingly convincing, but it does throw doubt on
the popular view that evolution is a steady but slow progression from
small brains to larger.  It is just as likely that we are accidents,
with long plateaus in our evolution broken, perhaps, by improbable
transitions.  I find this view more inspiring.  If we exist as the
result of some string of fluky accidents then this makes us "more"
unique.  More like the joker or wildcard in the pack, than the
inevitable result of evolution.

We need no Darwinian evolutionary validation for self-justification.  We
are our own justification.  The forces that shaped our creation are (1)
debatable and (2) irrelevant to our non-biological future.

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

