Max More writes:
>I have also supported the pursuit of a gradual integration of
>ourselves with our technology (transbiomorphosis) over the idea of a
>sudden, discontinuous uploading.  Not that I dispute the possibility
>of uploading, just that I suspect a gradual process to be more
>probable, and its prospect to be less likely to generate expectations
>of an idyllic post-upload life (as in uploading = heaven).
>Fortunately, the description of life as uploaded persons presented by
>the most well known upload enthusiast (Hans Moravec) sound nothing
>like the lazy, easy life of the Christian heaven.

I could also hardly be accused of painting a heaven-like picture of
uploading, but I do expect a rather sudden transition upon the arrival
of full uploading abilities.  Before this point, no matter how much
silicon we wrap meat in, the speed and cost of reproduction will be
limited by the meat part.  The speed at which our brains can run, and
the environments which we can survive in would also stay limited.
With fully artificial hardware, these and other limitations will
finally be removed, with great consequence.  I tried to explain myself
in more detail in my "If Uploads Come First" article recently posted
here.

