From: goodwin@snug.demon.co.uk (Tim Goodwin)
Date: Mon, 24 Jan 94 00:07:59  GMT
Subject: [#94-1-442] META: meat

Lurk, lurk, pounce!

I am interested in the Extropian approach to the eating and marketing of meat.

Recent posts have discussed the relationship between humans and animals and the 
possibility of a relationship between sentient animals and humans in the 
future.  There has also been some discussion about eating various kinds of 
animals, ranging from pets to human babies, and the ethical (for want of a 
better term) questions this raises.
 
It seems to me that there are a number of issues raised by the eating and 
marketing of animal products that could be discussed at greater length.  First 
of these is the question of whether the consumption of meat fits in with 
Extropian Principles (TM) at all.  I am sure that this will raise some hackles; 
in my experience  carnivores can become overly defensive, to the extent that 
they will sometimes view the simple statement, "I am a vegetarian", as a direct 
attack on their life-styles and morals.
 
I do not want simply to address this area, but rather to pose some general 
questions about the nature of an "Extropian" relationship with animals, and to 
comment on the nature of the debate regarding Self-Transformation.
 
Let us look at the present situation.  It would seem self evident that the 
marketing of meat as a product for mass consumption in the industrialised 
world does engender some pain and degradation to large numbers of animals,
particularly those "manufactured" in factory units.  Regardless of the possible
moral and ethical values (if any) involved in this process, there  remains some
interesting statistics regarding the use of resources.

Roughly two-thirds of the grain grown in the world goes to feeding animals so 
that we may eat meat.  While meat is eaten in the affluent countries of the 
west, some of the other inhabitants of our planet starve. 

In the same way that those scientists and engineers among us would be offended 
by an inefficient machine, should we not also be offended by an inefficient
market?

Then there is the issue of the nature of the marketing and the methods used by 
the meat industry to market and package their product as if it grew on trees in
neat cling-film packaging rather than come from living, breathing, life forms.

Most Extropians are rightly concerned with the issues of health and life
extension.  There is a growing body of evidence which suggests that 
vegetarians are less prone to heart disease and to cancers of the bowels and 
intestines.
 
The nature of our relationships with animals has come under scrutiny in other 
areas as well.  Should we, for example, support the deliberate infliction of
pain and death on animals for the amusement of humans, even if there is a 
market for it.  Bullfighting is a sport which does just that.  Should it be
supported in an Extropian society?  The free-market ideology, presented very
ably by various list contributors while I have been lurking, worries me when 
questions of this nature arise.

Lastly, let us consider the nature of "Self- Transformation".  Most of the
contributions I have seen so far are of a mechanistic, technical or 
biochemical nature, and very interesting they have been.  What I have not seen
a lot of, is discussion of the non-mechanical ways of self-transformation
exemplified best by Robert Anton Wilson in many of his publications.  Yoga,
meditation, ritual, self-hypnosis and drug assisted re-programming can also be 
valuable tools in the journey onward and outward.  Maybe a re-evaluation of our 
relationships with farmed food animals could prove to be equally rewarding.

I do not want to attack carnivores nor question their morals or life-styles.
I simply want to raise the issues.  I am not a vegetarian myself.  I am not a
scientist or engineer, I discovered Extropianism by reading Extropy in a
science fiction bookshop.  As an actor (my profession) I am interested in
fostering a discussion on the role of the artist in an Extropian society, and
breaking down the ridiculous barriers between science and the arts.


