From: vincek@netcom.com (Vince Kerchner)
Date: Sun, 23 Jan 1994 18:46:28 -0800
Subject: [#94-1-448] LEGAL: Announcement of PPL1 Policy

Well folks, I guess I volunteer to be the first person sanctioned by
the new Number 2.

Before that, I want to thank Harry Hawk for his stewardship of the
List as ombudsman for the past 1-2 years, and for the 2 very nice
parties he hosted at the SF Expo this year and last.  It was largely
through his kind and generous efforts as ombudsman that I was
encouraged to join the List, and I am very sorry to see him leave his
post so soon afterward.

I find Simon's first official post as "legal manager" to be somewhat
heavy-handed, and I am worried that an era of close-mindedness,
monitored by an arbitrary thought police, may be at hand.  In direct
contravention to Simon's explicit orders not to discuss this topic
publicly, I am going to discuss this topic publicly.  My guess is that
after this post, I may not be permitted on the List again.

Simon says:

>I will implement what I consider an appropriate punishment (suspension
>from the List for some period of time).  If I consider the behavior
>especially egregious, I will skip the warning and implement a
>punishment immediately.

I guess I would prefer a public posting of "crimes" and corresponding
"punishments", rather than depending on the List legal manager's
benevolence and largess to determine these relationships after the
fact.  Similarly, I would ask that a public distinction be made in
advance between those crimes for which a warning will be issued, and
those for which one will not be forthcoming.

>Listening to charges, which must be sent to me *privately*.  I will
>ignore charges posted to the List and will consider the annoyance
>caused thereby to nullify the charges themselves.

I am very uncomfortable with the directive to "do everything
*privately*" with the legal manager.  If charges are not to be posted
publicly, then the alleged perpetrator has no indication that her
conduct is in question, until punishment is dispensed.  Nor does she
have recourse to confront her accuser or to defend herself in any
reasonable way.  This sounds very much like a prescription for "secret
trials".  Furthermore, one crime does not nullify another any more
than two wrongs make a right.

>I will eventually punish A if A continues to annoy me with bogus
>charges.

This sounds very personal and arbitrary.  I think that the legal
manager must expect to take all charges seriously, and cannot exercise
"selective enforcement", as is so popular with contemporary gendarmes.

>I intend to punish *all* violations, regardless of "who started it"

Oh yes, let's be sure to punish the victims along with the criminals!
Or better yet, let's "kill them all and let God (or the legal manager)
sort it out."  This reminds me of how third grade teachers try to deal
with fights at recess.  However, I think we may want to act a bit more
responsibly.

>Like all of my judgements, ejection will be done at my discretion, and
>I will not discuss the matter with those whose advice I have not
>solicited.

Again, I would suggest a preposted list of crimes which merit
expulsion.  Perhaps such a list should even be put to a vote?  In
addition, I would hope that there would be a process by which
expulsions could be appealed.  Such appeals should certainly be open
to members of the List, and might even be mediated by jury.

>For the latter reason, you will not see any "Charges have been filed
>against... " messages from me on the List.  If I take action
>(suspension or, more rarely, ejection) against somone, I will announce
>the action *after* taking it, along with my reason. The announcement
>should be construed as an attempt to convey information about the kind
>of behavior I consider actionable, and *not* as an attempt to initiate
>discussion about legal issues in general or about my judgement in
>particular.

Again, I object to the above because it advocates secrecy.  I think
that the List legal manager should be responsible to the List members,
not the other way around.  Actions should be posted before
implementation, discussion should always be welcomed, and the
decisions of the legal manager should be openly questioned whenever
they are deemed inappropriate.

>Please address any questions about these matters to me *privately*.  I
>will not answer such questions if you post them to the List.

This is absurd.  In fact, I would propose that a List legal manager be
required to post an ongoing thread in which she fields and responds to
specific questions from List members, and in which open public
discussion of List legal issues is encouraged.

